A build in another store copies the derivations from the eval store
without asking for a signature check.

`copyDrvsFromEvalStore` copied each derivation's closure into the build
store with `copyClosure`'s default, `CheckSigs`. The build store then
refused any input the eval store held unsigned, or under a key the build
store does not trust: "cannot add path ... because it lacks a signature
by a trusted key". `nix build --eval-store auto --store ssh-ng://...`
failed so on a store-path source that had come from a binary cache the
build store does not trust (measured with pynixd on sg1, 2026-11-14).

The build hook copies the same inputs to a remote builder with
`NoCheckSigs` (`build-remote.cc`). The daemon on the other end still
checks an untrusted client: `daemon.cc` drops `dontCheckSigs` unless the
client is trusted. So the request changes nothing for an untrusted
client, and a trusted one gets what the build hook gives it.

It is meant for upstream.

--- a/src/libstore/remote-store.cc
+++ b/src/libstore/remote-store.cc
@@ -654,7 +654,9 @@
                 },
                 i.raw());
         }
-        copyClosure(*evalStore, *this, drvPaths2);
+        /* As the build hook copies inputs (`build-remote.cc`): the daemon
+           still checks the signatures of an untrusted client. */
+        copyClosure(*evalStore, *this, drvPaths2, NoRepair, NoCheckSigs);
     }
 }

